---
import { getCollection } from 'astro:content';
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';

const byOrder = (a: { data: { order: number } }, b: { data: { order: number } }) => a.data.order - b.data.order;
const research = (await getCollection('projects', (p) => p.data.featured && !p.data.draft)).sort(byOrder);
const allWriting = (await getCollection('writing', (w) => !w.data.draft)).sort(byOrder);
const writing = allWriting.filter((w) => !w.data.personal).slice(0, 3);
const personal = allWriting.filter((w) => w.data.personal);
const meta = (w: (typeof allWriting)[number]) => [w.data.venue, w.data.role].filter(Boolean).join(' · ');

// a project links to its blog post if it has one, otherwise to its entry on /research
const href = (p: (typeof research)[number]) =>
  p.data.links.find((l) => !l.url.startsWith('TODO'))?.url ?? `/research/#${p.id}`;
---

<Base title="Ioana Frîncu (Frincu): research on AI evaluation and safety" jsonLd>
  <header class="hero">
    <img class="portrait" src="/photo.jpg" width="168" height="168" alt="Portrait of Ioana Frîncu in a black bucket hat" />
    <div>
      <h1 class="name">Ioana Frîncu</h1>
      <p class="links" id="contact">
        <a href="https://github.com/flifenstein">GitHub</a>
        <a href="https://www.linkedin.com/in/frincui">LinkedIn</a>
        <a href="https://scholar.google.com/citations?user=Y2m2NaoAAAAJ&hl=en">Scholar</a>
        <a href="https://flifenstein.com">Blog</a>
        <a href="/cv/">CV</a>
        <span>f_ioana [at] tuta [dot] com</span>
      </p>
      <p>
        I want to know when we can trust what an AI evaluation tells us. Right now that means evaluation
        awareness and LLM deception, models that act differently when they think they are being tested and coding
        agents that quietly read the answer key. It's an itch I can't stop scratching, I work on it in a SPAR project
        and in my own studies, and I plan to apply to PhD programs in fall 2027, for 2028 entry.
      </p>
      <p>
        By day I'm a product manager at aistack, imec's AI systems team in Ghent, where we run coding agents on real
        hardware and measure what they cost, how fast they go and whether their scores hold up. That's also why I'm
        exploring technical AI governance on the compute side next to safety research. Long term I'd like to be a
        research manager, but first my inner child wants a proper go at being a researcher and sticking with one
        problem.
      </p>
      <p>
        Before imec I studied HCI and NLP at Twente and Aalto and worked at Microsoft's AI for Good Lab. Off the clock
        I hike long trails and play a gnome warlock.
      </p>
    </div>
  </header>

  <section class="questions" aria-labelledby="questions">
    <h2 id="questions">Questions I want to work on</h2>
    <ul class="plain">
      <li>When a model says "this is a test" in its reasoning, does it act differently, and does awareness it never voices change behaviour too?</li>
      <li>Why does one sentence of prohibition stop coding agents from peeking? Is that compliance, or noticing that someone is watching?</li>
      <li>When a model explains what it did ("I remembered the fix"), how do we check it, and what does it mean when it is wrong?</li>
      <li>What can the hardware side verify about how a model was run, without seeing the weights or the data?</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <div class="cards">
    <section class="card" aria-labelledby="research">
      <h2 id="research">Research</h2>
      <ul class="plain">
        {
          research.map((p) => (
            <li>
              <a href={href(p)}>{p.data.title}</a>
              <span class="meta">{p.data.venue ?? p.data.context}, {p.data.year}</span>
            </li>
          ))
        }
      </ul>
      <p class="mono"><a href="/research/">All research →</a></p>
    </section>

    <section class="card" aria-labelledby="writing">
      <h2 id="writing">Writing</h2>
      <p class="label">At aistack, while working at imec</p>
      <ul class="plain">
        {
          writing.map((w) => (
            <li>
              <a href={w.data.url}>{w.data.title}</a>
              <span class="meta">{meta(w)}</span>
            </li>
          ))
        }
      </ul>
      <p class="label sep">Personal blog</p>
      <ul class="plain">
        {
          personal.map((w) => (
            <li>
              <a href={w.data.url}>{w.data.title}</a>
            </li>
          ))
        }
      </ul>
      <p class="mono"><a href="/writing/">All writing →</a></p>
    </section>
  </div>
</Base>
